{
  "slug": "masculino-femenino",
  "number": 2,
  "part": 1,
  "title": "Masculine and feminine",
  "tagline": "Articles, endings that give gender away and the words most learners get wrong.",
  "goal": "Work out the gender of many nouns from their ending, learn the common exceptions and put adjectives and job titles into the feminine.",
  "minutes": 12,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "articulos",
      "title": "Articles",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every French noun is **masculine or feminine**, even objects and ideas. English has nothing like it, so the gender has to be learned with each word. The article shows it:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "",
            "Masculine",
            "Feminine",
            "Before a vowel or silent h",
            "Plural"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**Definite** (the)",
              "*{le} livre*",
              "*{la} table*",
              "*{l'}ami, {l'}école*",
              "*{les} livres*"
            ],
            [
              "**Indefinite** (a, some)",
              "*{un} livre*",
              "*{une} table*",
              "*{un} ami, {une} école*",
              "*{des} livres*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "Learn every noun with its article",
          "text": "*l'* and *les* hide the gender. That's why it pays to memorise *un ami* and *une école*: the indefinite article always shows it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "terminaciones",
      "title": "Clues from the ending",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "There's no perfect rule, but these endings are right most of the time."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "Usually masculine",
          "head": [
            "Ending",
            "Examples",
            "Common exceptions"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**-age**",
              "*le fromage, le voyage, le garage*",
              "*la page, la plage, l'image (f), la cage*"
            ],
            [
              "**-ment**",
              "*le moment, l'appartement, le monument*",
              "—"
            ],
            [
              "**-eau**",
              "*le gâteau, le bateau, le chapeau*",
              "*l'eau (f), la peau*"
            ],
            [
              "**-isme**",
              "*le tourisme, le réalisme*",
              "—"
            ],
            [
              "**-oir**",
              "*le miroir, le soir, le couloir*",
              "—"
            ],
            [
              "**-ier / -er**",
              "*le cahier, le dîner, le papier*",
              "*la mer*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "Usually feminine",
          "head": [
            "Ending",
            "Examples",
            "Common exceptions"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**-tion / -sion**",
              "*la nation, la natation, la télévision*",
              "—"
            ],
            [
              "**-té**",
              "*la liberté, la beauté, la santé*",
              "*l'été (m), le côté*"
            ],
            [
              "**-ure**",
              "*la voiture, la culture, la confiture*",
              "*le murmure*"
            ],
            [
              "**-ette**",
              "*la baguette, la chaussette, la fourchette*",
              "*le squelette*"
            ],
            [
              "**-ance / -ence**",
              "*la chance, la science, la patience*",
              "*le silence*"
            ],
            [
              "**-ie**",
              "*la boulangerie, la vie, la pluie*",
              "*le parapluie, le génie*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "Many words in *-tion* and *-té* look like English words in -tion and -ty, and they are feminine: *la nation*, *la liberté* (liberty), *la beauté* (beauty)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "trampas",
      "title": "Everyday words that are easy to get wrong",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These very common words often trip learners up. Speakers of Spanish or Italian get them wrong too, because in those languages they have the **opposite gender**."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "French",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*le lait* (m)",
              "milk"
            ],
            [
              "*le sel* (m)",
              "salt"
            ],
            [
              "*le sang* (m)",
              "blood"
            ],
            [
              "*le doute* (m)",
              "doubt"
            ],
            [
              "*le légume* (m)",
              "vegetable"
            ],
            [
              "*la mer* (f)",
              "sea"
            ],
            [
              "*la dent* (f)",
              "tooth"
            ],
            [
              "*la couleur* (f)",
              "colour"
            ],
            [
              "*la douleur* (f)",
              "pain"
            ],
            [
              "*la minute* (f)",
              "minute"
            ],
            [
              "*la planète* (f)",
              "planet"
            ],
            [
              "*la méthode* (f)",
              "method"
            ],
            [
              "*l'équipe* (f)",
              "team"
            ],
            [
              "*la fin* (f)",
              "end"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "Words ending in *-eur* like *couleur* and *douleur* are feminine, but *le bonheur* (happiness) and job titles like *le chanteur* are masculine. Learn each one with its article."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "adjetivos",
      "title": "Feminine adjectives",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In French, adjectives **agree** with the noun: they change for feminine and plural."
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "masculine + -e = feminine",
          "caption": "*grand → grand{e} · petit → petit{e} · joli → joli{e}*"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Sometimes the *-e* makes the final consonant **audible** (*petit* «ptee» → *petite* «pteet»); sometimes nothing changes (*joli* = *jolie*). If the adjective already ends in *-e*, it doesn't change: *rouge, facile*."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Pattern",
            "Masculine → feminine"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**-eux → -euse**",
              "*heureux → heureuse · sérieux → sérieuse*"
            ],
            [
              "**-if → -ive**",
              "*sportif → sportive · créatif → créative*"
            ],
            [
              "**-er → -ère**",
              "*cher → chère · premier → première*"
            ],
            [
              "**-el → -elle**",
              "*naturel → naturelle · cruel → cruelle*"
            ],
            [
              "**-en → -enne**",
              "*italien → italienne · ancien → ancienne*"
            ],
            [
              "**-on → -onne**",
              "*bon → bonne · mignon → mignonne*"
            ],
            [
              "**-il → -ille**",
              "*gentil → gentille*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "title": "Irregular",
          "text": "*beau → belle · nouveau → nouvelle · vieux → vieille · blanc → blanche · long → longue · doux → douce · frais → fraîche · fou → folle*"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "personas",
      "title": "People and animals",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Job titles and nationalities also have a feminine form, where English often uses the same word (a singer, a teacher)."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Pattern",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**+ e**",
              "*ami → amie · étudiant → étudiante · français → française*"
            ],
            [
              "**-eur → -euse**",
              "*chanteur → chanteuse · danseur → danseuse*"
            ],
            [
              "**-teur → -trice**",
              "*acteur → actrice · directeur → directrice*"
            ],
            [
              "**-er → -ère**",
              "*boulanger → boulangère · infirmier → infirmière*"
            ],
            [
              "**-ien → -ienne**",
              "*musicien → musicienne · chien → chienne*"
            ],
            [
              "**-on → -onne**",
              "*lion → lionne · patron → patronne*"
            ],
            [
              "**Different word**",
              "*homme → femme · roi → reine · garçon → fille · coq → poule*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Notice the nasal [[1]]: *lion* and *italien* are nasal, but *lionne* and *italienne* aren't."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
